<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick William Koko</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1898">1898</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as King Koko</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick William Koko was king of Nembe, also called Brass, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niger-river">Niger</a> delta of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a>, from 1889. A Christian who later returned to the traditional religion, he led the raid on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-niger-company">Royal Niger Company</a>'s depot at Akassa in 1895 in protest at the company's trade monopoly. After a British punitive expedition he withdrew into the interior, where he died in 1898.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-william-koko.jpg" alt="Frederick William Koko" width="320" height="239" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Signed (on prow of canoe) by Robert Barnes ARWS (1840–1895) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_Koko_in_His_War_Canoe.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
